Phase C: generic page-body editor (services, branches, static pages)

Lets the admin override the body of any service / branch / static
content page with a Quill rich-text editor + image upload, without
touching the bundled HTML.

ARCHITECTURE
- public.pages — new Supabase table keyed by URL path. A row exists
  only when the admin has overridden a page; missing rows mean the
  hardcoded HTML stays as the source of truth. Fully additive.

- assets/js/runtime.js — new applyPageOverride() runs on every page
  load. If a row matches the current URL path AND the page has a
  <div data-page-body>, the bundled HTML inside that div is replaced
  with the DB body_html. Page title + meta description are also
  swapped if set.

- admin/pages.html (NEW) — page picker grouped by category (Services,
  Branches, Top-level, Legal). Click a page to edit; saved rows get
  a gold dot in the sidebar. Editor has the same Quill toolbar as
  the blog (headings, lists, alignment, link, image upload) plus
  title + meta-description fields. "Revert to default" deletes the
  override.

- admin.js — listPages / loadPage / savePage / deletePage helpers.

INSTRUMENTATION
- 30 HTML pages got <div data-page-body> markers via
  tools/add-page-body-marker.js: all service pages, all 10 branch
  pages, plus about/contact/careers/corporate/gift-voucher/menu/
  offers/branches/blog/before-after/gallery/book.

  Pages that don't have a page-hero section (services index, legal
  pages) were skipped — they'd need a manual marker before they can
  be overridden. Listed for future cleanup.

DEPLOY
1. Run supabase/migrations/20260519300000_pages.sql in the Supabase
   SQL editor. Idempotent.
2. Upload the changed files to Hostinger.
3. Visit /admin/pages, pick a page, type content, Save & publish.

NOTE
The admin/banners page is functionally a Site Config editor
(it edits content_sections['site']) — renaming the nav label to
"Site config" is a UI-only cleanup, deferred to keep this commit
focused.

